    <section id="question-promo" class="section">
      <div class="section__head">
        <h2>The Question</h2>
        <p>
          <code>&lt;QuestionPromo question /&gt;</code>. The sidebar card for The Question, the
          fortnightly community survey and live discussion Ben runs alongside Redwoods. It has two
          states, chosen by one field: while the survey's <code>answerBy</code> deadline is in the future
          it promotes the open question, and afterwards it falls back to the mailing list. The artwork
          and the link to past episodes both point at Ben's page either way.
        </p>
        <p>
          It reads <code>src/data/the-question.ts</code> by default, which is the only file that changes
          when a new question goes live — there is no step to take one down. Because the site is statically
          built and nothing rebuilds it the moment a survey closes, the deadline is re-checked in the
          browser by an inline script in the document head, which can only ever move the card from open
          to closed. Deciding it there rather than beside the markup is what stops a closed question flashing
          on screen. Pass <code>question</code> explicitly — as the two demos below do — to render a fixed
          state that ignores all of that.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h3 class="subhead">Question open</h3>
      <div class="question-promo-demo"><QuestionPromo question={questionOpenSample} /></div>

      <h3 class="subhead">Question closed</h3>
