          attribution. Like the header, it's mounted once by the layout shell and identical on every
          page. In the shell it renders on the cream card surface — full-width beneath the grid at
          desktop, and flush at the bottom of the joined column below that.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="footer-demo"><Footer /></div>
    </section>

    <section id="toc" class="section">
      <div class="section__head">
        <h2>Table of contents</h2>
        <p>
          <code>&lt;TableOfContents headings minDepth maxDepth /&gt;</code>. A rail of page headings
          for long-form articles, fed by Astro's <code>headings</code> from <code>render()</code>.
          Nesting is carried by indentation against a single hairline, and the section you're
          currently reading is marked by an accent segment of that rail. Defaults to h2 through h4.
        </p>
        <p>
          Where the <code>main</code> column is at least 960px wide it renders as the sticky rail below;
          narrower than that it collapses to a closed disclosure, so the list stays reachable on a phone
          without pushing the article down. The active marker is progressive enhancement — an <code
            >IntersectionObserver</code
          > sets <code>aria-current</code>, and with scripting off every link still works.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h3 class="subhead">Wide column</h3>
      <p>
        Fed with this page's own sections, so the marker tracks as you scroll. On an article this
        column is sticky and sits in the gutter beside the prose.
      </p>
      <TableOfContents headings={sections.map((s) => ({ depth: 2, slug: s.id, text: s.label }))} />

