    </section>

    <section id="layout" class="section">
      <div class="section__head">
        <h2>Layout</h2>
        <p>
          Every page uses one responsive shell of four regions — <code>header</code>,
          <code>main</code>, <code>aside</code>, and <code>footer</code> — owned by
          <code>Page.astro</code>. The <code>.shell__*</code> wrappers own the card surface — grid placement,
          the breakpoint-dependent join <code>border-radius</code>, the cream fill, and clipping —
          because only the shell knows where each card sits in the grid. Content styling (padding,
          type) lives on the inner elements (<code>.main-wrap</code>, <code>.footer</code>,
          <code>.sidebar</code>), and every one of them pads with <code>--space-lg</code> — the same token
          as the shell gutter and the column gap, so the inset inside a card matches the space between
          cards. The <code>header</code> is the exception: its chrome lives on
          <code>.header</code> because <code>position: fixed</code> at tablet must be on the element itself.
          Pages provide <code>main</code> content (default slot); <code>header</code>,
          <code>aside</code>, and <code>footer</code> are global and identical on every page. The aside
          is <code>Sidebar.astro</code>. A page that wants the full width opts out with
          <code>&lt;Layout aside=&#123;false&#125;&gt;</code>, which drops the region from the grid
          entirely rather than leaving an empty card — this page is the only one that does.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h3 class="subhead">Breakpoints</h3>
      <p>
        Driven by two breakpoints. These are raw <code>px</code> values rather than tokens because CSS
        custom properties cannot be used inside <code>@media</code> conditions.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>&lt; 850px</strong> — single column: header, main, aside, and footer stacked and flush,
          reading as one rounded card.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>850–1300px</strong> — the header becomes the rail: a fixed-width (<code
            >--layout-rail</code
          >), full-viewport-height card pinned with
          <code>position: fixed</code>, while the right column (main + aside + footer) scrolls past
          underneath as one joined card.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>&gt; 1300px</strong> — the aside leaves the joined column and becomes a third column
          of its own (<code>--layout-aside</code>), with the footer spanning main and aside beneath
          them. All three become free-standing rounded cards. The shell grows fluidly to
          <code>--layout-shell-max</code>, centered beyond that.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        1300px is a deliberate trade, and the cost is worth knowing before anyone
        <q>fixes</q> it. The rail, the aside, the column gaps and the main column's padding come to roughly
        944px, so main only clears <code>--layout-prose-max</code> from about 1616px up. Splitting the
        aside off at 1300px means main crosses that boundary well short of the measure — 830px down to
        356px at the switch, taking the hero back through its container query with it. That is accepted:
        three columns on a 1440px laptop is the shape this layout is for, and a narrow-but-readable main
        column is the price. Raising the breakpoint to about 1620px buys the measure back; narrowing the
        rail and aside to roughly 600px combined buys both.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>

